  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, sorry things are so slow today. I checked your desktop and disk space looks fine, so that isn't the problem. A few quick questions so I can narrow it down: 1) Is it only the shared drives that are slow, or are websites, Teams and email slow too? 2) Are you at the warehouse or office, or working from home over VPN? 3) Are coworkers near you also having slow shared drives or internet right now? 4) Is it one shared folder in particular? If so, which one? While you're getting back to me, here are a few things worth trying: restart the desktop (a full restart) if it's been on for days, and pause any big uploads or downloads like OneDrive sync. If you're on VPN, disconnect and reconnect it. Also, our management system currently shows your desktop as offline, so please check that the network cable is plugged in firmly. If it turns out the whole site is slow, reply and say so and we'll pass it to our network team right away.",
  "internal_note": "User: James O'Connor (id 8), Operations, Warehouse Supervisor, hours 06:00-14:00 CT. Account active, no issues. Device BL-DT-0108 (desktop): 78% disk free (400/512 GB), no warnings. It shows offline, last check-in 2026-10-11T01:20Z, which could point to a network or agent problem. Disk space is ruled out (KB-017 doesn't apply). Following KB-015 quick checks, I asked the user: is it just shares or everything, on-site or VPN, are coworkers affected, is it one specific share. KB-015 says to escalate to Tier 2/network if the whole site is slow at once or one share is slow for everyone. Basic fix steps sent (restart, pause sync, reconnect VPN, check cable).",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-015",
    "KB-019"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
